answer:
## What the law said on 1 October 2025

The applicable document is **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version 110**, in force from **6 August 2025 to 24 November 2025**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- The property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The rental provider or agent must not **solicit or invite** an offer higher than the advertised rent. The penalty stated is **60 penalty units for an individual** or **300 penalty units for a body corporate**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Advertising the property at one rent and then representing that a different rent applied may also be **false or misleading advertising of rent**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The Act also prohibits misleading or deceptive conduct, or a false or misleading representation about the rent, where it is used to induce someone to enter a residential rental agreement. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

However, the documents do **not** establish that merely receiving or accepting an unsolicited higher offer was prohibited on 1 October 2025. The later version of the Act adds a separate prohibition on accepting an unsolicited higher offer, but that version was not in force until **25 November 2025** and therefore does not apply to your date. [[3]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf)

The documents also do not specifically say that falsely recording an applicant as having “withdrawn” is unlawful, or provide a specific procedure for challenging that event. They do state that the application form must be the prescribed form, and that applicants should contact the rental provider or agent to check the outcome. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can do and save

You can ask the agent, in writing, to:

1. confirm that you did **not** withdraw;
2. identify who recorded the withdrawal and when;
3. explain whether the property was offered at the original advertised rent or whether a higher rent was sought; and
4. correct any inaccurate record about your application.

Save:

- a complete copy or screenshot of the original advertisement showing the rent;
- your submitted application and any confirmation of submission;
- the email saying you withdrew;
- the full email headers or timestamps, if available;
- the later advertisement showing the higher rent;
- every message or note referring to another applicant offering more;
- your written response denying withdrawal; and
- a dated chronology of events.

The retrieved documents do **not** prescribe a particular evidence list or state exactly which body must receive a complaint. They do, however, support raising the matter as possible prohibited higher-rent solicitation or false/misleading rent advertising, depending on what the communications show. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
